        <title type="html"><![CDATA[Remove parseHTMLUnsafe for Safari]]></title>
        <id>https://github.com/mdn/browser-compat-data/pull/27392</id>
        <link href="https://github.com/mdn/browser-compat-data/pull/27392"/>
        <updated>2025-07-24T06:09:50.000Z</updated>
        <content type="html"><![CDATA[<img src="https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/1198915?u=5dabca633afe77afbfb17bfcf7221a1d9931685f&v=4" width="64" height="64" alt=""/><br/><h4 dir="auto">Summary</h4>
<p dir="auto">I'm removing parseHTMLUnsafe for Safari, as the implementation is so broken that it can't really be used in real-life.</p>
<h4 dir="auto">Test results and supporting details</h4>
<p dir="auto">The main goal of the parseHTMLUnsafe API is to be able to parse HTML containing declarative shadow root (which can't be done using innerHTML). Unfortuantely, when the declarative shadow dom contains web components (which will happen 99% of the time), Safari currently does not call the constructor and connectedCallback of those parsed web components, making the feature simply unusable.</p>
<p dir="auto">I've reported the bug to Webkit (<a href="https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=296420" rel="nofollow">https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=296420</a>) but for now it's safer to basically say that support is non-existant on Safari as the main use case is not working.</p>]]></content>
        <author>
            <name>bakura10</name>
            <email>bakura10@noreply.github.com</email>
            <uri>https://github.com/bakura10</uri>
        </author>
        <published>2025-07-24T03:29:16.000Z</published>
    </entry>
